17592186045417] [17592186446477 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446477 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446477 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446477 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446477 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e8f37801-7fba-5407-99f3-56966d38068b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446478 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446478 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446478 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446478 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f768c06e-68ed-5c8a-96fe-b36cf58285fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446479 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446479 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446479 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446479 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+2$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(1,0)\\in D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446479 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "004a7cc4-06c2-5abf-9f5d-0dfb86998289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446479 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(1)+2 \\\\  & =2+0-4+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =2(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0+8+2 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0-8+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $18$ at the point $(-2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446480 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446480 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446480 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446480 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{3}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x\\}.$ \nYou are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,0),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446480 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11bccf6f-4ced-5e74-80c9-222565fe2e6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446480 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region: \n\n![](images/21/21edc0f327ce949027b6cfb26789442ea0f7a3be7d7c9638d540dff77062870a.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(1,0)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0-1)^{3}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(1,1) & =(1-1)^{3}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446481 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/85/856b131ba85b8fd1da0050ead917d5757b4b4eea46aca54bef7d7b103a896bc6.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\le 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22939b6a-86a1-5a24-b491-90aeb5c6c381" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446481 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve  ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x,\\,-2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=0 \\\\ -2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(0,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,0).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,0),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (-2,0).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-(t)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-{t}^{2})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,0),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (2{t}^{2}-3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 4t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution, $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (-2,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain three candidates on the boundary:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(0,2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-3$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-3$ at the points $(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $5$ at the point $(-2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446482 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446482 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446482 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4xy+1$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 8\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49397200-f106-5784-934d-a685f0e1e2bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446482 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-4y,\\,2y-4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y=0 \\\\ 2y-4x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+1 \\\\  & =8{cos}^{2}⁡t+8{sin}^{2}⁡t-32\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1 \\\\  & =9-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (9-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -32\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{8},0),\\,(2,2),\\,(-2,2),\\,(-2,-2),\\,(2,-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{8},0)$ | $(2,2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(-2,-2)$ | $(2,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ | $-7$ | $25$ | $-7$ | $25$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $25$ at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(2,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446483 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446483 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446483 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446483 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+4x$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(-2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(0,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57601ebb-8cf3-5513-9b66-f85999e2eebc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446483 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2y+4,\\,2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2y+4=0 \\\\ 2x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the point $(0,-2).$ Since $(0,-2)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema. It lies on the boundary of $D,$ which we consider in the next step.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(0,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,0).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =2(t)(0)+4(t) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-2,0).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,-4)$ and $(0,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-4,0).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =2(0)(t)+4(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (-4,0).$\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(0,-4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-2,0⟩+t(⟨0,-4⟩-⟨-2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨-2,0⟩+t⟨2,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2+2t,-4t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(-2+2t,-4t) \\\\  & =2(-2+2t)(-4t)+4(-2+2t) \\\\  & =-16{t}^{2}+24t-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-16{t}^{2}+24t-8)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -32t+24 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one candidate point, $(-\\frac{1}{2},-3).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(0,-4),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-3)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},-3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-8$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the point $(-2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446484 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446484 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446484 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446484 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global minimum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x-y$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,1)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446484 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8052460c-148b-5893-b35a-1e1c771c0935" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446484 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/7f/7fe1aa21de9b6354e561eb1e82f6c83b25c71770470dc8501e6b491e35e2c391.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,1)$ is in the interior of $D$, we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(2,2),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =(1)(1)-1-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,0) & =(0)(0)-0-0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)(0)-2-0 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(2,2) & =(2)(2)-2-2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,2) & =(0)(2)-0-2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0)$and $(0,2),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446485 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(-1,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-4,0)$, and $(4,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8bdc5f30-6e9d-55a3-aacc-08dbc13064bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446485 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/dc/dc5a440d527fcf7583c4a341f08f2c6b5f6b4c1dc87b75286ea0a7f569d6babe.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(-1,0)$ lies inside $D,$ we include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-4,0),\\,(4,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4,0) & =-4{e}^{-4}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}} \\\\ f(4,0) & =4{e}^{4}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4} \\\\ f(-1,0) & =-{e}^{-1}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-\\frac{1}{e}$ at the point $(-1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4{e}^{4}$ at the point $(4,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446486 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fb41ea9-db84-5bfb-a8a6-5486c5782ac8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446486 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x,\\,-2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=0 \\\\ -2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+3 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-4{sin}^{2}⁡t+3 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the points $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(2,0)$ and $(-2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$ You are given that the function has no critical points." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2f4f1da-3ab7-5268-ad0e-cfd1ed45e614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince f has no critical points in D , the list of candidates for the extrema won't contain critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\le 1$, this equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval,  $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain candidate points: \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(0,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86706" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9c/9ce1b06ac9fc3caeed0a1f7866511be42b4c127a0bdddfd40b74e211ed24e02a.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e240afca-8d54-5fc1-a704-b3948ecfdb13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2},\\,4xy\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4xy=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=R \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the entire segment of solutions $\\{(x,0)\\,:\\,x\\in [0,2]\\}.$ Since all these points do not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include them in the list of candidates for extrema (they lie on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider them in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =2(t)(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =2(0)(t)^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(0,2)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2(t)(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2} \\\\  & =8t-2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (8t-2{t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 8-6{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2(4-3{t}^{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ inside the interval $t\\in (0,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one  candidate point, $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{9}$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the points on the line segment connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0),$ and on the line segment connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{9}$ at the point $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=3xy$ on the closed elliptical region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\le 2\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e78c82da-b48f-5dab-aeb6-fcde924129f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3y,\\,3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3y=0 \\\\ 3x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{2},0),\\,(1,\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(-1,\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(-1,-\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(1,-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(1,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(-1,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(-1,-\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(1,-\\frac{1}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the points $(-1,\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(1,-\\frac{1}{3}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(1,\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(-1,-\\frac{1}{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446490 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{2}+y$ on the closed region \n$D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\}.$ \nYou are given  that $f$ has no critical points in the region and on the boundary of the region,  the points that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $(0,0)$, $(2,4),$ and  $(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2504620-1607-5645-b4d2-e55a27da4e3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446490 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nNotice that we are told that $f$ has no critical points in the region.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,4),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0-2)^{2}+0 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(2,4) & =(2-2)^{2}+(4) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(1,1) & =(1-2)^{2}+(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2$ at the point $(1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4$ at the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
